  "answer_text": "**\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\nThis quote is a famous observation by the 14th-century Arab sociologist, historian, and philosopher **Ibn Khaldun**, written in his monumental 1377 work, the ***Muqaddimah*** (also known as the *Prolegomena*). \n\n### The Context Behind the Quote\nIn the *Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun dedicated a significant amount of analysis to economics, taxation, and the role of government. This specific quote highlights his strong opposition to **rulers and states engaging directly in commerce and trade**. \n\nIbn Khaldun argued that when a government or a \"wealthy ruler\" enters the marketplace as a competitor, it spells disaster for the economy for several reasons:\n\n1. **Unfair Competition and Coercion:** Rulers do not play by the same rules as private merchants. Because they hold political and military power, rulers can force farmers or producers to sell goods to them at artificially low prices, and then force citizens to buy those same goods at inflated prices. \n2. **Crowding Out the Private Sector:** Since private merchants cannot compete with the absolute power and deep pockets of the state, they are driven out of business. \n3. **Loss of Tax Revenue:** As private merchants disappear and the economy shrinks, the state's overall tax base erodes. Ironically, by trying to make a direct profit through trade, the ruler ultimately destroys the tax revenue that keeps the state alive.\n4. **Lack of Efficiency:** Rulers lack the specialized discipline, risk management, and organizational skills of professional merchants. They do not have to worry about bankruptcy in the same way, leading to waste, corruption, and systemic inefficiency.\n\n### Why Organized Merchants Surpass Rulers\nIbn Khaldun asserted that businesses run by \"responsible and organized merchants\" will always be superior in the long run. Private merchants survive and thrive based on **merit, customer satisfaction, accountability, and careful organization**. Because their personal livelihood is on the line, they must optimize their supply chains, treat customers fairly, and manage risks carefully. \n\nBecause Ibn Khaldun formulated these principles nearly four centuries before Adam Smith published *The Wealth of Nations*, he is often celebrated by modern historians as a major forerunner to free-market economic theory.",
